From cf81efd8ed6bfc285726c37f96dd4fbedb783e8d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 21:52:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 084/113] rust: add xxhash and random abstractions

Two small bindings for kernel facilities that Rust drivers currently reach
by declaring the C symbol themselves or calling into `bindings` directly.

`xxhash::xxh64()` wraps `lib/xxhash.c`'s 64-bit hash. Chaining calls by
passing the previous result as the next seed lets a caller fingerprint
scattered regions -- the rows of an image tile -- without first copying
them into one contiguous buffer.

`random::fill_bytes()` wraps `get_random_bytes()`, the CSPRNG source for
keys and nonces. There was no safe way to obtain random bytes at all, so
callers used `bindings::get_random_bytes()` in an unsafe block.

Both consumers are in the vino DisplayLink driver: xxhash fingerprints each
64x16 strip to find frame damage, and random generates the HDCP session
nonces and keys.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-0
Assisted-by: Codex:gpt-5
Signed-off-by: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
